From 8168b21902c4a12467fd1049404ecbfbcacb1a3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:16:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] server: answer clients in parallel

`handle_events` ran inline on the loop that also drives `rpc.sync()`, one client
after another. On a pruned node a single request can reach for a block the node has
discarded, which costs seconds rather than milliseconds, so one wallet with deep
history stalled indexing and every other connected client behind it.

Split the batch in three. Connects and disconnects mutate the peer map, so they stay
serial, and the clients with work are taken out of the map so each task owns one
outright. Answering runs across them in parallel, each task touching only its own
`Peer` and a shared `&Rpc` -- the same split `notify_peers` has always relied on.
Survivors go back in the map serially.

Behaviour is unchanged per client: its requests are answered in one batch, in the
order they arrived, a write error still disconnects it, and a disconnect still
discards whatever was queued behind it. The folding that carries those two ordering
rules is now `fold_messages`, which needs no live `Rpc` and so has tests.

Also corrects the write-timeout rationale in `accept_loop`, which described writes
happening on the server loop. They no longer do, and a wedged peer now holds a
worker instead -- still worth bounding, for a different reason than before.
